<GuidePage
  slug="secret-santa-gift-ideas"
  description="Secret Santa gift ideas sorted by budget (under ₹300, ₹500, ₹1000 and ₹2000, or $5 to $30) and by person, plus tips for choosing a gift for a colleague you barely know."
  intro="You drew a name, and now you have to find something they will actually like, on a budget, possibly for someone you’ve only spoken to in meetings. These ideas make it easier."
>
  <section class="panel">
    <h2>How to choose a Secret Santa gift</h2>
    <p>
      The best Secret Santa gifts are not the most expensive. They are the ones that show you paid a little attention.
      Before you shop, spend five minutes on these questions:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>What do they talk about?</strong> A hobby, a sports team, a favourite show, their pet, their plants.</li>
      <li><strong>What is on their desk or in their photos?</strong> Lots of coffee? Always cold? Never has a pen?</li>
      <li><strong>Did they share a wish list?</strong> If so, use it. Surprises are nice, but a wanted gift is nicer.</li>
      <li><strong>What should you avoid?</strong> Food allergies, diet choices, religious practices and things they already have plenty of.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If you know nothing at all, aim for something <strong>useful, consumable or cosy</strong>. Those three categories
      suit almost anyone and never end up gathering dust.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <h2>Gifts under ₹300 or $5</h2>
    <p>
      Budgets are shown in rupees and the typical matching budget in dollars, not exact conversions. Small budgets are
      common in offices and colleges. A neat wrap and a handwritten note go a long way.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>A pack of good pens or gel pens</strong> with a small notebook.</li>
      <li><strong>Gourmet chocolate bar or a box of homemade cookies.</strong></li>
      <li><strong>A succulent or small plant</strong> in a simple pot.</li>
      <li><strong>Fun socks</strong> in a festive pattern.</li>
      <li><strong>A pack of flavoured tea bags or instant coffee sachets.</strong></li>
      <li><strong>A cute keychain or bag charm</strong> linked to something they love.</li>
      <li><strong>Sticky notes in fun shapes</strong> for the person who leaves notes everywhere.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <h2>Gifts under ₹500 or $10</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>A scented candle</strong> in a calm scent like vanilla or sandalwood.</li>
      <li><strong>A quirky coffee mug</strong> (unless their wish list says no more mugs).</li>
      <li><strong>A desk plant</strong> that is hard to kill, such as a money plant or snake plant.</li>
      <li><strong>A phone stand or cable organiser</strong> for the tidy-desk fan.</li>
      <li><strong>A small snack hamper:</strong> nuts, dried fruit, namkeen or chocolates in a basket.</li>
      <li><strong>Hand cream and lip balm set</strong> for winter.</li>
      <li><strong>A pocket puzzle or brain teaser</strong> for the person who loves a challenge.</li>
      <li><strong>A paperback book</strong> by an author or in a genre they mentioned.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <AdSlot name="inGuide" />

  <section class="panel">
    <h2>Gifts under ₹1000 or $20</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>An insulated water bottle or travel mug</strong> that keeps drinks hot or cold for hours.</li>
      <li><strong>A cosy throw or a pair of soft slippers</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>A card or board game</strong> that works for small groups.</li>
      <li><strong>A desk organiser or wooden pen stand</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>A gourmet food kit:</strong> hot chocolate set, specialty coffee beans or a spice box.</li>
      <li><strong>A plant with a nice ceramic pot</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>A journal or planner</strong> for next year.</li>
      <li><strong>Wireless mouse or a laptop sleeve</strong> for someone who works on the go.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <h2>Gifts under ₹2000 or $30</h2>
    <p>Larger budgets are more common among friends and family.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Bluetooth speaker or a basic pair of wireless earbuds</strong> from a trusted brand.</li>
      <li><strong>An experience:</strong> movie tickets, a café voucher, or a workshop like pottery or baking.</li>
      <li><strong>A hobby starter kit:</strong> painting, embroidery, gardening or a cookbook with key ingredients.</li>
      <li><strong>A personalised gift:</strong> a printed photo frame, an engraved key holder or a custom name mug.</li>
      <li><strong>A self-care box:</strong> bath salts, a face mask set, a candle and a fluffy towel.</li>
      <li><strong>A good-quality backpack or tote bag</strong>.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <h2>Gift ideas by person</h2>
    <h3>For the coffee or tea lover</h3>
    <p>Specialty beans, a pour-over filter, a tea sampler, a mug warmer or a set of biscotti to go with it.</p>
    <h3>For the foodie</h3>
    <p>A hot sauce or pickle sampler, a spice set, a fun cookbook, or homemade treats if you are a good baker.</p>
    <h3>For the plant parent</h3>
    <p>A new plant, a self-watering pot, a mister, or a small set of gardening tools.</p>
    <h3>For the gamer or puzzle fan</h3>
    <p>A card game, a jigsaw puzzle, a Rubik’s-style cube, a gaming mouse pad or a controller stand.</p>
    <h3>For the fitness fan</h3>
    <p>A shaker bottle, a resistance band set, a gym towel or a jump rope.</p>
    <h3>For the reader</h3>
    <p>A book they mentioned, a quirky bookmark, a book light or a reading journal.</p>
    <h3>For the person who has everything</h3>
    <p>
      Something consumable, such as good chocolates, a treat box or a candle, or an experience like a café voucher.
      Things that get used up never add to the clutter.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <h2>Gifts to avoid</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Anything too personal</strong>, like clothing in a specific size, perfume or underwear, especially at work.</li>
      <li><strong>Joke gifts that could embarrass</strong> someone in front of colleagues.</li>
      <li><strong>Food they can’t eat.</strong> Check for allergies, vegetarian or vegan diets and fasting days.</li>
      <li><strong>Alcohol</strong>, unless you know the person drinks and it is allowed in your group.</li>
      <li><strong>Re-gifted items</strong> that are obviously used or still have someone else’s tag.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <h2>Last-minute ideas</h2>
    <p>Forgot until the day before? It happens. These can be picked up quickly or sent instantly:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>An e-gift card for a café, bookstore or streaming service, with a nice printed note.</li>
      <li>A box of good sweets or chocolates from a local shop.</li>
      <li>A plant from the nearest nursery.</li>
      <li>A “coupon book” of favours, such as “I’ll get your coffee for a week” (works best among friends).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Still need to draw names? Our free <a href="/secret-santa-generator/">Secret Santa generator</a> gives each person
      a private link, and our <a href="/guides/secret-santa-rules/">Secret Santa rules guide</a> covers the whole setup.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel faq">
    <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
    <details>
      <summary>Should I include a gift receipt?</summary>
      <p>Yes, if the shop offers one. Tuck it into the card so they can exchange the size or colour without asking.</p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Is it okay to go over the budget?</summary>
      <p>
        A little over is fine; a lot over is not. A much bigger gift can make others feel their gifts look small. If you
        want to give more, make it more thoughtful rather than more expensive.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Should I sign the card?</summary>
      <p>
        Only if your group reveals Santas when gifts are opened. Otherwise write “From your Secret Santa” and label the
        gift with the recipient’s name.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>
</GuidePage>
